{
  "dom_id": "step-q131930",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$, where\n\n$$\nr(s)=\u27e8\\operatorname{sin}\u2061s,\\,1,\\,\\operatorname{cos}\u2061s\u27e9,\\,0\\le s<2\\pi .\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\n\u03ba(s)=\u2016{T}^{\u2032}(s)\u2016=\u2016{r}^{\u2033}(s)\u2016\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{\u2032}(s)$ and ${r}^{\u2033}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\u2032}(s) & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061s)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\operatorname{cos}\u2061s,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061s\u27e9 \\\\ {r}^{\u2033}(s) & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061s)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061s,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061s\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u03ba(s) & =\u2016{r}^{\u2033}(s)\u2016 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061s)}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061s)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}\u2061s+{cos}^{2}\u2061s} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
